Those numbers are already a pair of latitude/longitude coordinates.  
If you feed them into Google’s Geocoding API (or any reverse‑geocoding service) you’ll get the human‑readable address that sits at that point.

**Example request**

```
https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?latlng=37.4224764,-122.0842499&key=YOUR_API_KEY
```

**Typical response snippet**

```json
{
  "results": [
    {
      "address_components": [...],
      "formatted_address": "Google Building 41, 1600 Amphitheatre Parkway, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA",
      "geometry": {
        "location": {
          "lat": 37.4224764,
          "lng": -122.0842499
        },
        ...
      },
      ...
    }
  ],
  "status": "OK"
}
```

So the coordinates **37.4224764, –122.0842499** point to the Googleplex (Google’s headquarters) in Mountain View, California. If you need the exact address or additional place details, just run the above API call with a valid key.
